WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.140
A secure API architecture starts by identifying the moving parts.

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:13.400
Clients may include browsers, mobile apps, scripts, partner systems, backend services, automation jobs, and administrators.

3
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:24.460
Behind the API may be application services, databases, identity providers, message queues, file stores, analytics systems, and downstream third-party services.

4
00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:28.720
Each component has different privileges, assumptions, and failure modes.

5
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:35.080
Trust boundaries show where data, identity, control, or responsibility moves between systems.

6
00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:39.900
A request crossing from a client into an API gateway crosses a boundary.

7
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.640
A gateway forwarding to application code crosses another.

8
00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:48.260
Application code reading from a database or calling a downstream service crosses additional boundaries.

9
00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:55.800
Security decisions should be explicit at these points so the application does not accidentally inherit trust from a place that should only route traffic.

10
00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:59.920
Internal APIs should not automatically trust every request.

11
00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:08.660
Internal services can be misconfigured, compromised, called through unexpected paths, or reused outside their original design.

12
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:15.080
A private network location is useful context, but it is not a complete identity or authorization model.

13
00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:24.140
Service-to-service calls should still have clear caller identity, scoped permissions, input validation, logging, and monitoring appropriate to their risk.

14
00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:28.420
Centralized controls can help when they are difficult to bypass.

15
00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:37.800
Gateways, shared authentication middleware, reusable validation helpers, common authorization libraries, and standard logging patterns reduce inconsistency.

16
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:41.020
But centralization should not hide responsibility.

17
00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:51.280
Sensitive operations still need local context, especially for object ownership, tenant boundaries, workflow state, and business-specific authorization.

18
00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:56.740
Good architecture makes the safe path easy and the bypass path obvious during review.
